# datadog extension env template
#
# Datadog's official remote MCP server uses OAuth on first session launch —
# there is NO API token to paste here (unlike dash0, which uses a Bearer
# token). The only thing this .env configures is which regional endpoint
# the MCP server points at. The manifest's URL template is
# `https://mcp.SITE/api/unstable/mcp-server/mcp?toolsets=all` — `SITE` is
# substituted from this file at registration time.
#
# When this extension is enabled on a project, copy this file to
# `.indusk/extensions/datadog/.env` and set SITE to your Datadog site.
# The MCP server auto-registers on the next `indusk extensions enable
# datadog` (or `indusk update`).
#
# On first Claude Code session after registration, you'll be prompted to
# complete OAuth. The browser flow handles auth; nothing else lives here.

# --- Required: pick ONE site ---

# US1 (most US-based accounts — the default):
SITE=datadoghq.com

# US3:
# SITE=us3.datadoghq.com

# US5:
# SITE=us5.datadoghq.com

# EU1:
# SITE=datadoghq.eu

# AP1:
# SITE=ap1.datadoghq.com

# US1-FED:
# SITE=ddog-gov.com

# --- Toolset filtering (optional) ---
#
# The manifest URL hardcodes `?toolsets=all` — every available toolset
# (APM, Logs, Metrics, Monitors, Dashboards, Security Signals, Error
# Tracking, Feature Flags, DBM, LLM Observability, etc.). To narrow,
# edit `.indusk/extensions/datadog/manifest.json` and change the query
# string to a comma-separated subset, e.g. `?toolsets=apm,logs,metrics`.
# Valid toolsets vary by Datadog product enablement; see
# https://docs.datadoghq.com/bits_ai/mcp_server/setup/ for the current
# list. `all` works best with clients that do their own tool filtering
# (Claude Code does).
